denisdavidvalente@hotmail.com (Denis) wrote:

> I was wondering if people thought there was enough variety in artifact
> weapons in Nethack.  Some of the variants like slashem added some cool
> ones, but vanilla nethack has had pretty much the same weapons for
> quite some time now.

I think the problem isn't variety (other than too many longswords) but
rather variety of /useful/ artifacts.  The choice of which artifact
isn't a very interesting one.

I'd say balancing the artifacts would make them more interesting.  If
I had to arbitrarily change (non-quest) artifacts to make some more
appealing (and others less appealing), here are some ideas:

Grayswandir: 1.5x damage instead of double
Trollsbane: kills trolls instantly (or trolls hit/killed by it don't
regen?)
Dragonsbane: kills dragons instantly (or dragons always leave scales?)
Werebane: werefoo cannot summon help when wielded
Ogresmasher: kills ogres instantly
Demonbane: does 2X damage against riders, as well (or more??)
Tsurugi: always insta-kill engulfers
Grimtooth (I forget this even exists sometimes): always poisoned
Orcrist: 3X damage to orcs instead

I'm half tempted to implement these ideas in a patch.  I'm curious on
feedback on this.  My motivation is to make artifacts that nobody
really uses into something useful and to make the player have to make
more decisions.  Is this too unbalancing? What would it take for
people to use the artifacts that already exist without making them too
powerful? Not that every artifact must be equal, but certainly if they
were closer in power that it required a harder decision (or depended
upon your situation) then it would be more interesting.

To balance the large amount of "insta-kill" weapons (not that a
combat-wombat can't insta-kill most anything anyways by the end game),
I also propose a limit on artifacts found in the game to be a
predetermined random number from 3-6 (barring named, quest, and
invocation artifacts).

Also, what about the idea of slightly random to-hit and damage
bonuses? That way you wouldn't know exactly how good each weapon is. 
Maybe Excalibur would end up dealing the most damage on average per
turn in some game, but you wouldn't know that unless you tried out
several weapons and tested them.

